1. BOM — Browser Object Model

The Browser Object Model allows JavaScript to interact with the browser itself, outside the webpage's HTML.

The main object is:

window

Examples:

window.alert("Hello");
window.location.href = "https://google.com";
window.history.back();
window.innerWidth;
window.navigator.userAgent;

Common BOM-related objects/APIs include:

window
location
history
navigator
screen
browser dialogs such as alert(), confirm(), prompt()

Think:

Browser
   │
   └── window
        ├── location
        ├── history
        ├── navigator
        └── screen
2. DOM — Document Object Model

The Document Object Model represents the HTML document as a tree of objects.

For example, this HTML:

<html>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello</h1>
    <button>Click</button>
  </body>
</html>

is represented roughly like:

Document
   │
   └── html
        │
        └── body
             ├── h1
             │    └── "Hello"
             │
             └── button
                  └── "Click"

JavaScript can manipulate this tree:

const heading = document.querySelector("h1");

heading.textContent = "Hello Bhargav";

Or:

document.body.style.backgroundColor = "black";

So:

DOM is mainly about manipulating the webpage/document.

